  "etymologyNotes": [
    {
      "s": "makemeahanzi",
      "t": "An eye 巳 pierced by a dagger 戈, an old mark of slavery"
    },
    {
      "s": "dong_chinese",
      "t": "Pictograph of a line going into the eye. Relation to current meaning unclear."
    },
    {
      "s": "shuowen_jiezi",
      "t": "眾萌也。从古文之象。凡民之屬皆从民。",
      "w": 1
    },
    {
      "s": "wiktionary",
      "t": "Pictogram (象形): Later jiajie (假借) for 民 (OC *min, “the people”).\nShuowen does not have a glyph origin for this character.\nMany, including Shirakawa, have claimed that it represents an eye pierced by a dagger. Sometimes explained as the original form of 盲 (OC *mraːŋ, “blind”), but the phonetics differ too much for this to be believable.\nFrom Proto-Sino-Tibetan *r-mi(j)-n (“people, man, human being”). Cognate with Tibetan མི (mi) and Dzongkha མི (mi)."
    }
  ],
  "shuowen": "眾萌也。从古文之象。凡民之屬皆从民。",
